{"version":3,"term":{"cols":100,"rows":32},"timestamp":1793529870,"idle_time_limit":2.0,"command":"bash /home/claude/solves/narnia/L2.sh","title":"Narnia L2 -> narnia3: buf[128] strcpy overflow - C prototype, asm shellcode (nasm), gcc harness, then the exploit","env":{"SHELL":"/bin/bash"}}
[0.002, "o", "$ ssh narnia2@narnia.labs.overthewire.org -p 2226   # password 0h8Tj5fxbv\r\n$ cat /narnia/narnia2.c   # char buf[128]; strcpy(buf, argv[1]);\r\n$ objdump: buffer at ebp-0x80; saved ebp at +128, return address at +132\r\n$ readelf: GNU_STACK RWE (executable stack); ASLR off\r\n$ 1. write a C prototype of the payload logic, compile with gcc -m32, run it\r\n$ 2. write the same logic as x86 assembly in vim, assemble with nasm\r\n$ 3. objdump the object to see the encoding, objcopy to raw bytes, xxd them\r\n$ 4. compile a gcc harness that mmaps and runs those exact bytes\r\n$ 5. write the Python exploit that puts the bytes in argv[1] and sweeps\r\n$    return addresses until the PWN! marker appears\r\n"]
